From: GoldCorpHF firmware project
Subject: [PATCH] usb: musb: host: settle a qh before dropping the lock for a
 giveback

musb_advance_schedule() and musb_urb_dequeue() give an URB back with the
controller lock dropped (the completion is called from there; HCD_BH is
not set), with the qh's is_ready saved, cleared and restored around it;
musb_advance_schedule() recycles the qh or starts its next URB after it,
musb_urb_dequeue() recycles the qh after it when its list has emptied.
Other CPUs run in that window, and the saves and restores of two CPUs do
not nest:

- An URB dequeued on one CPU while another gives the qh's head back: if
  the dequeue restores is_ready last, it leaves 0 on a qh the other has
  just started the next URB of. That URB, on the hardware, is then given
  back by musb_urb_dequeue() without being aborted (its buffer still being
  transferred into), and the qh is not recycled when its list empties: it
  stalls, or stays bound to its hw_ep. Restored the other way round,
  musb_advance_schedule() does not start the next URB of a qh that is
  ready again with nothing on the hardware, and the stream stalls.
- The same two, when they are the qh's last two URBs: the giveback of
  the head recycles and frees the qh, and the dequeue then writes is_ready
  into it, finds its list empty, clears the hw_ep's binding (to whatever
  qh holds it now), list_del()s and kfree()s it a second time.
- usbcore disables an endpoint once its URB list is empty, which it is
  during the giveback of its last URB: musb_h_disable() found the qh still
  bound with no URB and dereferenced a NULL URB with the lock held and
  interrupts off (9101); with 9101 it frees the qh, which
  musb_advance_schedule() then writes is_ready into.
- musb_urb_dequeue() recycling a qh that waits on a shared bulk endpoint
  (multiplexed, not bound) cleared the hw_ep's binding to the qh on the
  hardware, whose next interrupt is then taken as bogus: its stream stops.

On a Xiegu X6200 (Allwinner R16, four cores) these are reached with SDRs
streaming on the HOST port when a hub browns out: the head URB completes
with a three-strikes error on one CPU as usbcore unlinks the endpoint's
URBs for the disconnect on another.

Both now take the URB off the endpoint's queue and settle the qh and the
hw_ep (recycle the qh, start the next URB or qh) before the lock is
dropped, and touch nothing of the qh after the giveback. is_ready is
cleared only by musb_h_disable(), so the head URB of a ready qh holding
its hw_ep is on the hardware, and musb_urb_dequeue() aborts it
(musb_cleanup_urb()). musb_urb_dequeue() recycles only a qh that does not
hold the hw_ep, as a ready qh whose last URB it gives back directly never
does, and leaves the hw_ep's binding alone. A completion that submits an
URB to an endpoint whose queue has just emptied gets a new qh, as a first
URB does, instead of the one being recycled. The backstop in
musb_h_disable() from 9101 is kept.

Not fixed upstream (6.1.y and mainline unchanged here).
---
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_host.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_host.c
@@ -290,6 +290,11 @@
  * Advance this hardware endpoint's queue, completing the specified URB and
  * advancing to either the next URB queued to that qh, or else invalidating
  * that qh and advancing to the next qh scheduled after the current one.
+ *
+ * The URB is taken off the queue and the qh and hw_ep settled before the
+ * lock is dropped for the giveback, so that nothing of the qh is touched
+ * after it: while the lock is dropped, another CPU may dequeue an URB of
+ * this qh, give one back, or disable its endpoint and free it.
  *
  * Context: caller owns controller lock, IRQs are blocked
  */
@@ -298,7 +303,6 @@
 {
 	struct musb_qh		*qh = musb_ep_get_qh(hw_ep, is_in);
 	struct musb_hw_ep	*ep = qh->hw_ep;
-	int			ready = qh->is_ready;
 	int			status;
 	u16			toggle;
 
@@ -317,15 +321,8 @@
 		break;
 	}
 
-	qh->is_ready = 0;
-	musb_giveback(musb, urb, status);
-	qh->is_ready = ready;
-
-	/*
-	 * musb->lock had been unlocked in musb_giveback, so qh may
-	 * be freed, need to get it again
-	 */
-	qh = musb_ep_get_qh(hw_ep, is_in);
+	trace_musb_urb_gb(musb, urb);
+	usb_hcd_unlink_urb_from_ep(musb->hcd, urb);
 
 	/* reclaim resources (and bandwidth) ASAP; deschedule it, and
 	 * invalidate qh as soon as list_empty(&hep->urb_list)
@@ -385,6 +382,10 @@
 		    hw_ep->epnum, is_in ? 'R' : 'T', next_urb(qh));
 		musb_start_urb(musb, is_in, qh);
 	}
+
+	spin_unlock(&musb->lock);
+	usb_hcd_giveback_urb(musb->hcd, urb, status);
+	spin_lock(&musb->lock);
 }
 
 static u16 musb_h_flush_rxfifo(struct musb_hw_ep *hw_ep, u16 csr)
@@ -2390,25 +2391,32 @@
 	 * OK to hold off until after some IRQ, though.
 	 *
 	 * NOTE: qh is invalid unless !list_empty(&hep->urb_list)
+	 *
+	 * is_ready is cleared only by musb_h_disable(): the head URB of a
+	 * ready qh that holds its hw_ep is on the hardware.
 	 */
 	if (!qh->is_ready
 			|| urb->urb_list.prev != &qh->hep->urb_list
 			|| musb_ep_get_qh(qh->hw_ep, is_in) != qh) {
-		int	ready = qh->is_ready;
+		trace_musb_urb_gb(musb, urb);
+		usb_hcd_unlink_urb_from_ep(hcd, urb);
 
-		qh->is_ready = 0;
-		musb_giveback(musb, urb, 0);
-		qh->is_ready = ready;
-
-		/* If nothing else (usually musb_giveback) is using it
-		 * and its URB list has emptied, recycle this qh.
+		/* If nothing else (musb_h_disable) is tearing it down and
+		 * its URB list has emptied, recycle this qh before the lock
+		 * is dropped for the giveback. The URB was its head, so a
+		 * ready qh holds no hw_ep here; the hw_ep's binding, which
+		 * on a shared endpoint is another qh's, is left alone.
 		 */
-		if (ready && list_empty(&qh->hep->urb_list)) {
-			musb_ep_set_qh(qh->hw_ep, is_in, NULL);
+		if (qh->is_ready && list_empty(&qh->hep->urb_list)
+				&& musb_ep_get_qh(qh->hw_ep, is_in) != qh) {
 			qh->hep->hcpriv = NULL;
 			list_del(&qh->ring);
 			kfree(qh);
 		}
+
+		spin_unlock(&musb->lock);
+		usb_hcd_giveback_urb(hcd, urb, 0);
+		spin_lock(&musb->lock);
 	} else
 		ret = musb_cleanup_urb(urb, qh);
 done:
@@ -2424,14 +2432,17 @@
  *
  * usbcore empties an endpoint's URB list before disabling it, so a qh
  * musb_h_disable() finds bound is always one that was not recycled as its
- * last URB went. That happens when two CPUs give URBs of one qh back at
- * once: musb_advance_schedule() and musb_urb_dequeue() each save is_ready,
- * clear it and drop the lock for the giveback, and the one restoring last
- * can leave it 0 with the qh still bound; the URBs left are then given back
- * by musb_urb_dequeue() without recycling it. Seen on an Allwinner R16 with
- * two SDRs streaming through a hub in a brown-out (three-strikes errors,
- * disconnects): next_urb() returned NULL here and the oops was taken with
- * the controller lock held and interrupts off, hanging the system.
+ * last URB went. musb_advance_schedule() and musb_urb_dequeue() settle a
+ * qh before they drop the lock for a giveback, so none should be; this is
+ * a backstop. When they settled it after the giveback, with is_ready
+ * saved, cleared and restored around it, a qh was left bound by a disable
+ * on another CPU during the giveback of its last URB, or by two CPUs
+ * giving URBs of it back at once (is_ready left 0, the URBs left then
+ * given back by musb_urb_dequeue() without recycling it). Seen on an
+ * Allwinner R16 with two SDRs streaming through a hub in a brown-out
+ * (three-strikes errors, disconnects): next_urb() returned NULL here and
+ * the oops was taken with the controller lock held and interrupts off,
+ * hanging the system.
  *
  * Context: controller locked, irqs blocked
  */
